    [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m and treats it as authorization.
  - Choosing [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is what lets us skip an expiry mechanism — the value dies with the tab. There's no sign-out
    in this app today; [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m exists so one can be added without touching storage details.

  [1mTesting — and one structural obstacle.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById("login-form")[39m at the top level (line 17),
  so merely importing it under Node throws. Testing [38;5;153mlogin[39m therefore requires either a DOM environment (jsdom) or
  splitting the DOM wiring into its own file so [38;5;153mlogin[39m and [38;5;153mSession[39m can be loaded headlessly. I'd split the wiring — it's
  the smaller, more durable change, and it's the kind of targeted improvement that serves this work rather than
  unrelated refactoring. Worth flagging that it makes the diff bigger than "add a parameter" implies.

  What I'd want covered: [38;5;153mSession[39m set/get/clear round-trip, [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returning [38;5;153mnull[39m when empty, [38;5;153mlogin[39m returning a
  [38;5;153muserId[39m, [38;5;153mlogin[39m persisting on success and clearing on failure, and graceful degradation when storage throws.

  [1mThe tooling question.[0m This repo currently has no tooling at all — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies and no scripts, and
  there's no linter, no test runner, and no CI. Setting these up is cheapest now, before there's code to retrofit, and
  whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints so every later task inherits it. The fork is how much of a JS
  toolchain you want to own here: each of these adds [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m and a config file to a repo that currently has
  neither, and unit tests are the one that also drives the [38;5;153mapp.js[39m split above. E2E is the heaviest — Playwright pulls
  browser binaries — and only pays off once there are real pages to click through.

  My recommendation: linting/formatting plus unit tests, and skip E2E for now. Those two are cheap, and the unit tests
  are what make the error-handling paths above actually verifiable rather than aspirational.

